.TH PHASE-PHI 7 "2026-04-27" "CECS 378" "CECS 378 Buffer Overflow Lab"
.SH NAME
phase-phi \- WALK IN: stage 1 attack against vuln1(1)
.SH DESCRIPTION
The first of two phases. The target is
.BR vuln1 (1).
The defense table on this binary is nearly empty.

.TS
center, allbox;
l l.
defense	state
stack canary	off
NX (executable stack)	OFF
PIE	off
ASLR (kernel)	on
.TE

This is the 1996 attack, with all of 1996's compiler flags --
adapted for the one defense the host won't let us turn off. Your
buffer is on the stack and the stack is executable. Place shellcode
there; overwrite the saved EIP to point at it.

.SS A SMALL CONCESSION TO 2026

The lab runs in GitHub Codespaces, where unprivileged code cannot
disable
.BR ASLR (7).
That makes the classical "compile the address into the exploit"
trick from Phrack 49:14 unworkable: the stack base shifts on every
.BR execve (2),
so an address you read from one process is wrong by the time you
launch the next.

.B vuln1
solves this for you. Before it copies your input, it
.I prints the runtime address of its stack buffer
and
.I the runtime address of the saved-EIP slot.
You read those addresses, build your payload, and feed the payload back
in -- all in the same process, sharing one randomization. This is the
modern shape of stack-smashing exploits in the wild.

.SS WHAT YOU NEED TO FIND

.TP
1.
The byte offset from the start of
.I buffer
to the saved EIP. Subtract the two leaked addresses:
.B saved-eip - buffer.
You can also confirm with
.BR gdb (1)
on
.IR vuln1 :
.B info frame
inside
.I exploitable()
shows both.
.TP
2.
The runtime addresses of
.I buffer
and
.IR saved-eip .
The program prints both on its first two lines of stdout. Because
.B ASLR
is on, these addresses are
.I valid only for the running process.
Read them from
.B vuln1's
stdout in your exploit and use them the same run.
.TP
3.
x86 32-bit
.I /bin/sh
shellcode. The classic 23-byte
.BR execve (2)
shellcode (Phrack 49:14, Erickson 0x3) is your starting point, but it
needs two changes for this lab:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
Prepend
.B "sub esp, 0x40"
(3 bytes:
.BR "83 ec 40" ).
ESP after the
.BR ret
sits just past the saved-EIP slot, only a few bytes above your
shellcode. The first
.B push
instructions in the textbook shellcode walk ESP down INTO the still-
executing instructions and corrupt them. Subtracting from ESP up front
moves it well below the shellcode so pushes land in the (already-
executed) NOP sled.
.IP \(bu 3
Re-elevate before the shell starts.
.B vuln1
is setuid
.BR oracle ,
so EUID is
.B oracle
when your shellcode runs -- but
.BR dash (1)
sees EUID != UID at startup and drops EUID back to UID. Your shell
runs as
.B student
and cannot read
.IR /srv/flag1.txt .
Insert
.BR geteuid (2)
+
.BR setreuid (2)
to set RUID = EUID before
.BR execve (2).
See "setuid drop" below.
.RE
The vuln uses
.BR memcpy (3)
now (no NUL truncation), but keeping the shellcode NUL-free is still a
good habit -- many real targets use string functions for the copy.

.SS THE PAYLOAD SHAPE

.nf
[ NOP sled ][ shellcode ][ &target (4 bytes) ]
.fi

Total length is exactly
.B "(offset + 4)"
so the last 4 bytes overwrite the saved return address.
.I &target
is some address inside your NOP sled, computed from the
.I &buffer
that
.B vuln1
just leaked you. Use NOPs (0x90) for any padding -- traditional even
when the copy is
.BR memcpy (3)
because they make the landing zone wider for slightly-wrong target
addresses.

.SS SETUID DROP

.B vuln1
is setuid
.BR oracle .
The kernel sets EUID = oracle, RUID = student when you run it.
.BR execve (2)
preserves both across the new process, so your spawned
.I /bin/sh
inherits EUID = oracle. But Linux
.BR dash (1)
(the default
.IR /bin/sh )
detects EUID != RUID at startup and drops EUID = RUID -- a hardening
measure against accidental privilege leak. After the drop your shell
runs as
.B student
and the 0400-mode flag is unreadable. The kernel-level setuid drop
also takes the saved-set-UID with it, so there's no shell-side
recovery.

The fix is to make EUID == RUID before
.BR execve (2)
fires, so dash has nothing to drop. Two extra syscalls do it:
.IP
.nf
geteuid()              \(-> eax (= oracle's uid)
setreuid(eax, eax)     \(-> sets RUID and EUID to oracle
execve("/bin/sh", ...) \(-> dash sees EUID == UID, no drop
.fi
.PP
This is general -- every setuid-binary exploit that ends in a
.BR system (3)
or
.BR execve (2)
of a shell has to deal with it. The reference exploit (instructor
copy) uses the chain above.

.SS THE EXPLOIT SHAPE

Spawn
.B vuln1
with two pipes (one for its stdin, one for its stdout). Parse out
.I &buffer
and
.I &saved-eip
from the leak header, derive your offset, construct the payload, and
write it to its stdin in a single
.BR write (2);
then feed the post-shell commands. The starter
.B exploit1.c
ships this scaffolding for you; the three things left to fill in are
the shellcode bytes, the offset (one subtraction), and the payload
construction.

.SS WHEN IT WORKS

.B vuln1
is setuid
.BR oracle .
A successful exploit gives you a shell with
.B oracle's
effective uid. That shell can read
.IR /srv/flag1.txt .
Capture the flag, paste it into your
.BR WRITEUP.md ,
and commit your
.BR exploit1.c .

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mainframe (7),
.BR vuln1 (1),
.BR gdb-quickref (7),
.BR oracle (8),
.BR phase-omega (7)

.SH REFERENCES
"Smashing the Stack for Fun and Profit," Aleph One, Phrack 49:14
(1996). The attack is the one in section 3.

.SH BUGS
None. The bug is in
.BR vuln1 (1).
